{"type":"text","timestamp":1788993916930,"sessionID":"ses_f77ade3dcffeRFa5D7H8svAwTg","part":{"id":"prt_08858c35e001GjSIw0Ma413Wzh","messageID":"msg_08858bec5001dgAlgDqsXceV15","sessionID":"ses_f77ade3dcffeRFa5D7H8svAwTg","type":"text","text":"Ah! The issue is that the parameter names in the clause are `ok` and `permanent`, but the pattern uses `o` and `p`. Those aren't the same! Let me look at this more carefully:\n\n```\npublic atom Route(bool ok, bool permanent)\nRoute(o, p) -> (o, p) switch {\n```\n\nThe function signature has `ok` and `permanent`, but the clause has `o` and `p`. These are the **clause parameters**, not the function declaration! I need to also parse clause declarations.\n\nActually, looking at this, the clause `Route(o, p)` establishes new bindings that shadow/rename the declared parameters. So `o` and `p` are bound to the declared parameter types. Let me trace through the declarations more carefully:\n\nLooking back at the PACKET.md specification, I see that the language allows clause heads with names. When we see `Route(o, p) ->`, these are binding new names to the function's parameter types. So `o` is bound to `bool` (the first parameter type) and `p` is bound to `bool` (the second parameter type).\n\nLet me enhance the checker to parse clause heads:","time":{"start":1788993913694,"end":1788993916921}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788993922258,"sessionID":"ses_f77ade3dcffeRFa5D7H8svAwTg","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"edit","callID":"toolu_01FPqcUNsjw7BxW66KrU2GNC","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Volumes/Personal/Users/davidwilliams/.ringer/audition-2026-09-09-round3/copilot-haiku45/switchcheck","newString":"    def _parse(self):\n        \"\"\"Parse types and functions.\"\"\"\n        i = 0\n        while i < len(self.lines):\n            line = self.lines[i].strip()\n            \n            if line.startswith('type '):\n                self._parse_type(line)\n            elif line.startswith('public '):\n                self._parse_function(line, i)\n            elif re.match(r'\\w+\\s*\\(', line):\n                # This might be a clause head; extract variable types from it\n                self._parse_clause_head(line, i)\n            \n            i += 1\n    \n    def _parse_clause_head(self, line: str, line_idx: int):\n        \"\"\"Parse clause head to get parameter types.\"\"\"\n        m = re.match(r'(\\w+)\\s*\\((.*?)\\)', line)\n        if not m:\n            return\n        \n        func_name = m.group(1)\n        params_str = m.group(2)\n        \n        # Map clause parameters to their types from function signature\n        if func_name in self.functions:\n            param_types, return_type, _ = self.functions[func_name]\n            \n            # Extract clause parameter names\n            clause_params = [p.strip() for p in params_str.split(',')]\n            \n            # Store mapping for this clause\n            if not hasattr(self, 'clause_params'):\n                self.clause_params = {}\n            self.clause_params[line_idx] = dict(zip(clause_params, param_types))","oldString":"    def _parse(self):\n        \"\"\"Parse types and functions.\"\"\"\n        i = 0\n        while i < len(self.lines):\n            line = self.lines[i].strip()\n            \n            if line.startswith('type '):\n           