{"type":"text","timestamp":1788994234910,"sessionID":"ses_f77ade3dcffeRFa5D7H8svAwTg","part":{"id":"prt_0885da021001B9lftBS9Op1f2Z","messageID":"msg_0885d9ab5001O0VbpqyOedZhyA","sessionID":"ses_f77ade3dcffeRFa5D7H8svAwTg","type":"text","text":"Aha! The `_infer_type` method doesn't know about pattern-bound variables. It only knows about literals. So when it sees `m`, it returns `None` instead of `int`.\n\nI need to extend `_infer_type` to use pattern binding information. But this requires context about what pattern was matched. This is tricky because the call check doesn't have that context.\n\nLet me think about a better approach. When I check calls in a switch arm, I should have the pattern available, so I can infer the type of variables bound by that pattern.\n\nActually, let me revisit the call check. It iterates over all lines, and each line with `=>` might contain a guard or a body. I need to extract which variables are bound in the pattern for that line.\n\nLet me rewrite `_check_calls` to be smarter about switch arms:","time":{"start":1788994232354,"end":1788994234898}}}
