{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788994685558,"sessionID":"ses_f77a0a10bffeFUJZZsTQ59ZTrI","part":{"id":"prt_088648a700016bxl1Y89mzK30R","messageID":"msg_0886485db001wKPfq8yaRGGjMy","sessionID":"ses_f77a0a10bffeFUJZZsTQ59ZTrI","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1788994693410,"sessionID":"ses_f77a0a10bffeFUJZZsTQ59ZTrI","part":{"id":"prt_088648a73001JEqsnDLV0tsxHN","messageID":"msg_0886485db001wKPfq8yaRGGjMy","sessionID":"ses_f77a0a10bffeFUJZZsTQ59ZTrI","type":"text","text":"Let me think about another potential issue: In c07 (rebinding), I'm checking that and it works. But what if the specification also forbids a bare pattern that matches a clause parameter value? Let me re-read that section:\n\nFrom PACKET.md Section 2:\n> \"To match against a value a name already holds, write `== name`. A bare name in a pattern introduces a name; `== name` matches the value that name is bound to.\"\n\nAnd:\n> \"So a head that repeats a bare name — `F(acc, acc)` — is an **error**, not an equality constraint: both are introductions, and the second rebinds what the first bound.\"\n\nSo the rule is: if a bare name in a pattern is already in scope (as a clause parameter), it's rebinding. That's what I check. But then:\n\n> \"An arm whose pattern is a bare name already in scope is `rebinding`\"\n\nAnd the example shows:\n```csharp\nPick(n, e) -> e switch {\n    n => :same,\n    _ => :other\n}\n```\n\nSo `n` is already a clause parameter, and the pattern `n` rebinds it. That's what I'm checking.\n\nLet me think about what else could be wrong. Maybe I need to be more careful about when things are errors vs warnings? From the spec:\n\n> \"An arm every earlier arm already covers is dead. That is `unreachable_arm`, and it is a **warning** rather than an error\"\n\nSo unreachable_arm is a warning, not an error. But I still output it, which is correct.\n\nLet me look more carefully at the unreachable detection. From the spec:\n> \"An arm every earlier arm already covers is dead.\"\n\nThis means an arm is unreachable if ALL values it matches are already matched by earlier arms. Currently, I only check for literal pattern duplicates. But what about cases like:\n- First arm: `_` (catch-all)\n- Second arm: anything (unreachable)\n\nOr:\n- First arm: `(true, _)` \n- Second arm: `(true, false)` (unreachable, subsumed)\n\nLet me check if I'm handling this properly. In my current code, I only mark as unreachable if it's a duplicate literal. But I should also check if it's subsumed by a wildcard or more general pattern.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. Looking at c04 (unreachable):","time":{"start":1788994685555,"end":1788994693393}}}
