{"type":"text","timestamp":1788994699867,"sessionID":"ses_f77a0a10bffeFUJZZsTQ59ZTrI","part":{"id":"prt_08864ae17001agIqwBfdkmDNjK","messageID":"msg_08864a9d7001iPZDywG0XXJsQE","sessionID":"ses_f77a0a10bffeFUJZZsTQ59ZTrI","type":"text","text":"This is a simple duplicate - the same literal `:placed` appears twice. This is what I check for. And I correctly output `unreachable_arm` for it.\n\nLet me think about what the held-out cases might test:\n\n1. Maybe switches with guards that affect exhaustiveness?\n2. Maybe multiple errors in one file?\n3. Maybe complex tuple patterns?\n4. Maybe nested structures?\n5. Maybe type inference edge cases?\n\nActually, looking at the spec again, I notice something about guards:\n\n> \"A guard the checker can read as a type operation refines the clause\"\n\nAnd:\n\n> \"A guard the compiler **cannot read** — one comparing two variables, say — is not this, and is never reported: an arm is judged on its pattern alone there\"\n\nThis suggests that guards can affect exhaustiveness! Let me re-read that section:\n\nFrom PACKET.md Section 3:\n> \"That is exhaustive over `int`, with no catch-all, because the checker carries real integer intervals.\"\n\nAnd it shows:\n```csharp\nClassify(n) when n < 10              -> :low\nClassify(n) when n >= 10 and n < 100 -> :mid\nClassify(n) when n >= 100            -> :high\n```\n\nThis is exhaustive because of the guards! The guards partition the int space. So I need to check exhaustiveness considering guards as well!\n\nBut looking at my current test cases, none of them seem to test this (c01-c13 don't have guards that affect exhaustiveness).\n\nLet me focus on another angle: what if there's an issue with how I'm detecting atom types vs open types? Let me check c02:","time":{"start":1788994694679,"end":1788994699860}}}
