{"type":"text","timestamp":1788994712395,"sessionID":"ses_f77a0a10bffeFUJZZsTQ59ZTrI","part":{"id":"prt_08864e2ef001LG5Q5kODyhLIiT","messageID":"msg_08864df28001WVDJFlC3ARbYRh","sessionID":"ses_f77a0a10bffeFUJZZsTQ59ZTrI","type":"text","text":"So `Verdict` is `:pass | :fail`, and the first pattern is `(:pass, n)`, which is a tuple. But `Verdict` is not a tuple type, it's a union of atoms. So this pattern is vacuous. I correctly detect this.\n\nHmm, let me think about what else could be wrong. Let me look at one more potential issue: rebinding detection. The spec says:\n\n> \"An arm whose pattern is a bare name already in scope is `rebinding`\"\n\nI check if a bare name matches a clause parameter name. But what if there's something else in scope? The spec also says:\n\n> \"A name bound by one arm's pattern is not in scope in another arm's body\"\n\nSo only clause parameters and the current arm's bindings are in scope. I think I'm handling this correctly.\n\nActually, let me re-read the vacuous_arm rule one more time. It says:\n\n> \"If the arm's pattern is not a member of the subject's type at all, no value can reach it no matter what the other arms do — so it is `vacuous_arm`\"\n\nSo the question is: is this pattern a member of the subject's type?\n\nFor c12:\n- Subject type is `Verdict = :pass | :fail`\n- Pattern `(:pass, n)` is a 2-tuple\n- Can a `Verdict` value match `(:pass, n)`?\n- No, because `Verdict` is not a tuple type\n\nBut wait, I'm also checking for patterns like atom literals that aren't in the type's cases. Let me trace through my _check_vacuous:","time":{"start":1788994708207,"end":1788994712373}}}
