{"type":"text","timestamp":1788994743919,"sessionID":"ses_f77a0a10bffeFUJZZsTQ59ZTrI","part":{"id":"prt_088655e52001gMhhQuVphPs2cG","messageID":"msg_088655954001B8bGwhl152Todx","sessionID":"ses_f77a0a10bffeFUJZZsTQ59ZTrI","type":"text","text":"Interesting! The first arm has pattern `n` with guard `n < 5`, and the second arm has pattern `>= 5` with no explicit variable. The second pattern `>= 5` is a relational pattern, which is discussed in the spec:\n\n> \"A span of integers is a relational pattern. ... Those four clauses are **exhaustive over `int`** with no catch-all\"\n\nSo a pattern like `>= 5` is valid! But I'm not handling this in my pattern parsing. Let me check what happens:\n\nPattern: `>= 5`\n- It doesn't match `r'^\\w+$'` (bare variable)\n- It doesn't start with `(` (tuple)\n- It doesn't start with `(:` (compound)\n\nSo for rebinding, I check if it's a bare variable, and `>= 5` is not, so no error. Good!\n\nBut for exhaustiveness, I'm only checking literal atom patterns. So `>= 5` is not added to `covered`. This means the switch would be marked as inexhaustive, which is WRONG!\n\nSo I need to handle relational patterns in exhaustiveness checking. But this is complex... The spec says relational patterns are complex interval operations. I can't fully implement this, but I could at least recognize patterns like `>= N`, `<= N`, `> N`, `< N` and handle the basic cases.\n\nLet me check if c07 currently works:","time":{"start":1788994739794,"end":1788994743913}}}
