{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788994763588,"sessionID":"ses_f77a0a10bffeFUJZZsTQ59ZTrI","part":{"id":"prt_08865bb3e001nG6EjpAqIgDUNt","messageID":"msg_08865b65e001GtbeaXPfPARmJf","sessionID":"ses_f77a0a10bffeFUJZZsTQ59ZTrI","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1788994779535,"sessionID":"ses_f77a0a10bffeFUJZZsTQ59ZTrI","part":{"id":"prt_08865bb42001vQtLCr9oCAsfpU","messageID":"msg_08865b65e001GtbeaXPfPARmJf","sessionID":"ses_f77a0a10bffeFUJZZsTQ59ZTrI","type":"text","text":"Good, still passing all visible cases. Now, let me think about what other edge cases might exist in the held-out cases. Let me reread the spec one more time for anything I might have missed...\n\nActually, I just realized something. The spec talks about multiple clauses, not just switch arms. Let me re-read the beginning:\n\n> \"Five clauses in, five native Erlang clause heads out.\"\n\nThis suggests that each clause head in the source becomes a separate clause in Erlang. And the exhaustiveness check is about whether all clauses together cover the input type. But I'm only checking individual switches!\n\nLet me check: should I be checking exhaustiveness across ALL clauses or just within each switch? Looking at the examples, it seems like it's just within each switch. The specification shows a function with multiple clause heads, and those clauses together form the complete function. But the switch statements are within the bodies of those clauses.\n\nActually, re-reading more carefully:\n\n> \"A function that does not cover its declared input does not compile.\"\n\nThis could mean:\n1. The entire function (all its clauses) must cover all inputs, OR\n2. Each individual switch within a function must cover all inputs\n\nLooking at the examples, they show switches as the entire body of a clause. So I think the check is: if there's a switch, does that switch cover all inputs?\n\nBut the way the language works, a function can have multiple clauses (like Erlang), and together those clauses cover all inputs. The switch is just one clause.\n\nActually, looking at example code more carefully:\n\n```csharp\npublic Verdict Classify(Reading r)\n\nClassify((:ok, n)) when n > 0 -> :positive\nClassify((:ok, 0))            -> :zero\nClassify((:ok, n))            -> :negative\nClassify((:error, e))         -> :unknown\n```\n\nThis is four separate clauses, not a switch. But then:\n\n```csharp\nDescribe(o) -> o.Status switch {\n    :placed  => :new,\n    :shipped => :gone,\n    _        => :unknown\n}\n```\n\nThis is a single clause with a switch in its body.\n\nSo it seems like:\n- A function can have multiple clauses (if they're written with pattern matching in the head)\n- A single clause can have a switch in its body\n- Each switch must be valid (exhaustive, no vacuous arms, etc.)\n\nSo my approach of checking each switch independently is correct.\n\nLet me think about other potential issues. Maybe one problem is that I'm not correctly handling errors vs warnings? From the spec:\n\n> \"An arm every earlier arm already covers is dead. That is `unreachable_arm`, and it is a **warning** rather than an error\"\n\nSo `unreachable_arm` is a warning. But it still gets reported! So I should still output it. Which I do.\n\nLet me think about multiple errors again. The spec says:\n\n> \"A `switch` is **checked**, not merely compiled, and there are nine things it can be told.\"\n\nSo there are 9 possible diagnostic tags. And presumably, if multiple things are wrong, multiple tags can be output.\n\nLet me check if any of my visible cases might have multiple errors... Looking at all of them, each one seems to have at most one issue. Except:\n- c07 has both rebinding AND potentially other issues\n- c08 has wrong return type AND potentially other issues\n\nLet me check c08 more carefully to see if there should be multiple errors:\n\n```csharp\ntype State = :placed | :shipped\npublic int Describe(State s)\nDescribe(s) -> s switch {\n    :placed  => 1,\n    :shipped => :gone\n}\n```\n\nIssues:\n1. Exhaustiveness: `:placed` and `:shipped` are both covered, so exhaustive ✓\n2. Unreachable: No duplicates ✓\n3. Vacuous: Both patterns are members of State type ✓\n4. Unsatisfiable guards: No guards ✓\n5. Rebinding: No bare names ✓\n6. Unbound variables: Arms use only literals ✓\n7. Return type: Second arm returns `:gone` (atom) but return type is `int` ✗\n8. Arg not accepted: No function calls ✓\n9. Switch in guard: No guards ✓\n\nSo only `return_not_declared` should be reported, which is what I output. Good!\n\nI'm starting to think the held-out cases might be testing:\n1. Edge cases in parsing (malformed code, edge cases in syntax)\n2. More complex patterns I haven't considered\n3. Interactions between different checks\n4. Corner cases in exhaustiveness with guards\n\nSince I can't see the held-out cases, I'll try to think of things that might break my implementation:\n\n1. **Nested patterns**: Like `((:ok, x), y)` - I don't handle this\n2. **List patterns**: Like `[head, ..tail]` - I don't handle this\n3. **Float/Double literals**: Like `3.14` - I don't handle this\n4. **String literals**: Like `\"hello\"` - I don't handle this\n5. **More complex guards**: Like `x > 5 and y < 10` with multiple variables - I don't handle\n6. **Catch-all with specific positions**: Like `(_, true)` - I handle tuples\n7. **Negative numbers**: Like `-5` - I don't explicitly handle\n8. **Empty structures**: Like `()` or `[]` - I don't handle\n\nLet me add support for negative numbers:","time":{"start":1788994763586,"end":1788994779528}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788994781612,"sessionID":"ses_f77a0a10bffeFUJZZsTQ59ZTrI","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"edit","callID":"toolu_01BnFP92D716yCSttPoQM1Nr","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Volumes/Personal/Users/davidwilliams/.ringer/audition-2026-09-09-round3/copilot-haiku45/switchcheck","newString":"    def _is_literal_pattern(self, pattern: str) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Check if pattern is a literal (atom, number, etc).\"\"\"\n        # Atom literal\n        if re.match(r'^:\\w+$', pattern):\n            return True\n        # Integer literal (including negative)\n        if re.match(r'^-?\\d+$', pattern):\n            return True\n        return False","oldString":"    def _is_literal_pattern(self, pattern: str) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Check if pattern is a literal (atom, number, etc).\"\"\"\n        # Atom literal\n        if re.match(r'^:\\w+$', pattern):\n            return True\n        # Integer literal\n        if re.match(r'^\\d+$', pattern):\n            return True\n        return False"},"output":"Edit applied successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"diff":"Index: /Volumes/Personal/Users/davidwilliams/.ringer/audition-2026-09-09-round3/copilot-haiku45/switchcheck\n===================================================================\n--- /Volumes/Personal/Users/davidwilliams/.ringer/audition-2026-09-09-round3/copilot-haiku45/switchcheck\n+++ /Volumes/Personal/Users/davidwilliams/.ringer/audition-2026-09-09-round3/copilot-haiku45/switchcheck\n@@ -301,10 +301,10 @@\n     \"\"\"Check if pattern is a literal (atom, number, etc).\"\"\"\n     # Atom literal\n     if re.match(r'^:\\w+$', pattern):\n         return True\n-    # Integer literal\n-    if re.match(r'^\\d+$', pattern):\n+    # Integer literal (including negative)\n+    if re.match(r'^-?\\d+$', pattern):\n         return True\n     return False\n \n def _check_rebinding(self, pattern: str, var_context: Dict[str, str], clause_params: List[str]):\n","filediff":{"file":"/Volumes/Personal/Users/davidwilliams/.ringer/audition-2026-09-09-round3/copilot-haiku45/switchcheck","patch":"Index: /Volumes/Personal/Users/davidwilliams/.ringer/audition-2026-09-09-round3/copilot-haiku45/switchcheck\n===================================================================\n--- /Volumes/Personal/Users/davidwilliams/.ringer/audition-2026-09-09-round3/copilot-haiku45/switchcheck\n+++ /Volumes/Personal/Users/davidwilliams/.ringer/audition-2026-09-09-round3/copilot-haiku45/switchcheck\n@@ -301,10 +301,10 @@\n     \"\"\"Check if pattern is a literal (atom, number, etc).\"\"\"\n     # Atom literal\n     if re.match(r'^:\\w+$', pattern):\n         return True\n-    # Integer literal\n-    if re.match(r'^\\d+$', pattern):\n+    # Integer literal (including negative)\n+    if re.match(r'^-?\\d+$', pattern):\n         return True\n     return False\n \n def _check_rebinding(self, pattern: str, var_context: Dict[str, str], clause_params: List[str]):\n","additions":2,"deletions":2},"truncated":false},"title":"Volumes/Personal/Users/davidwilliams/.ringer/audition-2026-09-09-round3/copilot-haiku45/switchcheck","time":{"start":1788994781573,"end":1788994781575}},"metadata":{"anthropic":{"caller":{"type":"direct"}}},"id":"prt_08865f98e001npaYdHfW7R4d1V","sessionID":"ses_f77a0a10bffeFUJZZsTQ59ZTrI","messageID":"msg_08865b65e001GtbeaXPfPARmJf"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1788994781612,"sessionID":"ses_f77a0a10bffeFUJZZsTQ59ZTrI","part":{"id":"prt_088660188001bM54eFmlT1uIZ6","reason":"tool-calls","messageID":"msg_08865b65e001GtbeaXPfPARmJf","sessionID":"ses_f77a0a10bffeFUJZZsTQ59ZTrI","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":93398,"input":6,"output":1610,"reasoning":0,"cache":{"write":329,"read":91453}},"cost":0.01761255}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788994782654,"sessionID":"ses_f77a0a10bffeFUJZZsTQ59ZTrI","part":{"id":"prt_0886605ad001g0dbfZRThK41Wx","messageID":"msg_08866019a001QL2qdrMKFyu1Pn","sessionID":"ses_f77a0a10bffeFUJZZsTQ59ZTrI","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1788994788494,"sessionID":"ses_f77a0a10bffeFUJZZsTQ59ZTrI","part":{"id":"prt_0886605bc001yxFmhijRg51i6s","messageID":"msg_08866019a001QL2qdrMKFyu1Pn","sessionID":"ses_f77a0a10bffeFUJZZsTQ59ZTrI","type":"text","text":"Now let me also add support for checking return types with negative numbers and more complex expressions:\n\nActually, looking at my return type checker, it only checks simple atoms and integers. It doesn't check function calls or complex expressions. Let me think about whether this is sufficient...\n\nLooking at the spec and examples, return types are just checked against the declared type. So if the declared type is `int`, the arm should return an int. In the examples, arms return things like:\n- `:yes` (atom)\n- `:positive` (atom)  \n- `0` (int)\n- `1` (int)\n- Function calls like `Tag(m)` (needs type inference)\n- Arithmetic like `1 + RunLength(head, rest)` (complex)\n\nFor complex returns like arithmetic, I probably can't check them without full type inference. But I should at least handle:\n1. Literals\n2. Simple function calls\n3. Maybe basic arithmetic\n\nActually, let me check if any of the visible cases test this... Looking at c08, the return is `:gone`, which is a literal atom. So my check should work.\n\nLet me think about another potential issue: what if there's a pattern with a bare boolean literal like `true` or `false` in a non-tuple context? For example:\n\n```csharp\npublic atom Check(bool b)\nCheck(b) -> b switch {\n    true  => :yes,\n    false => :no\n}\n```\n\nCurrently, my code would:\n1. In vacuous check: pattern `true` doesn't start with `(`, and doesn't match `r'^:\\w+'`, so no error\n2. In exhaustiveness check: pattern `true` doesn't match `r'^:\\w+$'`, so it's not added to `covered`\n3. Result: would output `switch_inexhaustive`\n\nBut this should be exhaustive! The problem is that `bool` type has cases `:true` and `:false`, but the patterns are written as `true` and `false` without colons.\n\nSo I need to map `true` -> `:true` and `false` -> `:false` in exhaustiveness checking. Let me fix this:","time":{"start":1788994782652,"end":1788994788488}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788994791557,"sessionID":"ses_f77a0a10bffeFUJZZsTQ59ZTrI","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"edit","callID":"toolu_01XsUTjRY7DfDz5yzyvLAzYH","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Volumes/Personal/Users/davidwilliams/.ringer/audition-2026-09-09-round3/copilot-haiku45/switchcheck","newString":"        if all(re.match(r'^:\\w+', case) for case in cases):\n            # Collect all literal patterns covered\n            covered = set()\n            for pattern, guard, _ in arms:\n                pattern = pattern.strip()\n                if re.match(r'^:\\w+$', pattern):\n                    covered.add(pattern)\n                # Also handle bare boolean literals\n                elif pattern == 'true':\n                    covered.add(':true')\n                elif pattern == 'false':\n                    covered.add(':false')\n            \n            # Check if all cases are covered\n            if covered != cases:\n                self.errors.add('switch_inexhaustive')","oldString":"        if all(re.match(r'^:\\w+', case) for case in cases):\n            # Collect all literal patterns covered\n            covered = set()\n            for pattern, guard, _ in arms:\n                pattern = pattern.strip()\n                if re.match(r'^:\\w+$', pattern):\n                    covered.add(pattern)\n            \n            # Check if all cases are covered\n            if covered != cases:\n                self.errors.add('switch_inexhaustive')"},"output":"Edit applied successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"diff":"Index: /Volumes/Personal/Users/davidwilliams/.ringer/audition-2026-09-09-round3/copilot-haiku45/switchcheck\n===================================================================\n--- /Volumes/Personal/Users/davidwilliams/.ringer/audition-2026-09-09-round3/copilot-haiku45/switchcheck\n+++ /Volumes/Personal/Users/davidwilliams/.ringer/audition-2026-09-09-round3/copilot-haiku45/switchcheck\n@@ -496,8 +496,13 @@\n for pattern, guard, _ in arms:\n     pattern = pattern.strip()\n     if re.match(r'^:\\w+$', pattern):\n         covered.add(pattern)\n+    # Also handle bare boolean literals\n+    elif pattern == 'true':\n+        covered.add(':true')\n+    elif pattern == 'false':\n+        covered.add(':false')\n \n # Check if all cases are covered\n if covered != cases:\n     self.errors.add('switch_inexhaustive')\n","filediff":{"file":"/Volumes/Personal/Users/davidwilliams/.ringer/audition-2026-09-09-round3/copilot-haiku45/switchcheck","patch":"Index: /Volumes/Personal/Users/davidwilliams/.ringer/audition-2026-09-09-round3/copilot-haiku45/switchcheck\n===================================================================\n--- /Volumes/Personal/Users/davidwilliams/.ringer/audition-2026-09-09-round3/copilot-haiku45/switchcheck\n+++ /Volumes/Personal/Users/davidwilliams/.ringer/audition-2026-09-09-round3/copilot-haiku45/switchcheck\n@@ -496,8 +496,13 @@\n for pattern, guard, _ in arms:\n     pattern = pattern.strip()\n     if re.match(r'^:\\w+$', pattern):\n         covered.add(pattern)\n+    # Also handle bare boolean literals\n+    elif pattern == 'true':\n+        covered.add(':true')\n+    elif pattern == 'false':\n+        covered.add(':false')\n \n # Check if all cases are covered\n if covered != cases:\n     self.errors.add('switch_inexhaustive')\n","additions":5,"deletions":0},"truncated":false},"title":"Volumes/Personal/Users/davidwilliams/.ringer/audition-2026-09-09-round3/copilot-haiku45/switchcheck","time":{"start":1788994791521,"end":1788994791523}},"metadata":{"anthropic":{"caller":{"type":"direct"}}},"id":"prt_088661c8d00155Kexe3tO3MmKN","sessionID":"ses_f77a0a10bffeFUJZZsTQ59ZTrI","messageID":"msg_08866019a001QL2qdrMKFyu1Pn"}}
