{"type":"text","timestamp":1788994804680,"sessionID":"ses_f77a0a10bffeFUJZZsTQ59ZTrI","part":{"id":"prt_088664afd001OlfDjpxrTtykDA","messageID":"msg_088664641001P01dMeCZqdvuoz","sessionID":"ses_f77a0a10bffeFUJZZsTQ59ZTrI","type":"text","text":"Good, still passing all visible cases. Let me think about what else might be tested. Let me re-examine the spec one more time for subtle points I might have missed...\n\nActually, I just realized something about the catch-all rule. The spec says:\n\n> \"A **catch-all is legal only where the residual is open**\"\n\nAnd later:\n\n> \"Over a *closed* residual, where the compiler knows the missing case by name, `_` is an error telling you to name it\"\n\nSo a catch-all `_` on a closed type is an error. But what about catch-all on a type like `bool`, which is closed? Let me think...\n\nThe `bool` type has cases `:true` and `:false`. If there's a switch on a `bool` with a catch-all, that's an error!\n\nBut wait, I don't have any test cases with this. Let me check if any of my code handles this...\n\nIn my _check_exhaustiveness, I check:\n```python\nif has_catchall:\n    # Catch-all only legal on open types\n    self.errors.add('switch_inexhaustive')\n    return\n```\n\nBut I only do this if `subject_type_name == 'tuple' and subject_element_types`, which is tuple-specific. For atom types, I don't check this! Let me fix it:","time":{"start":1788994800381,"end":1788994804663}}}
