{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788994831827,"sessionID":"ses_f77a0a10bffeFUJZZsTQ59ZTrI","part":{"id":"prt_08866c5c90015nsmzuBChUg0mo","messageID":"msg_08866bfe7001IZ6q3SkS0Wl9Ok","sessionID":"ses_f77a0a10bffeFUJZZsTQ59ZTrI","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1788994837541,"sessionID":"ses_f77a0a10bffeFUJZZsTQ59ZTrI","part":{"id":"prt_08866c5cf0013r3794IeTgZx2Q","messageID":"msg_08866bfe7001IZ6q3SkS0Wl9Ok","sessionID":"ses_f77a0a10bffeFUJZZsTQ59ZTrI","type":"text","text":"Still good! Now let me think about what else might be wrong. Let me re-read the specification sections on each error one more time, paying special attention to details:\n\n### switch_inexhaustive\n> \"A switch must cover its subject. If some value of the subject's type matches no arm, that is `switch_inexhaustive`\"\n\nCheck: I verify that all cases are covered. ✓\n\n### unreachable_arm\n> \"An arm every earlier arm already covers is dead. That is `unreachable_arm`\"\n\nCheck: I check if a pattern was already seen. But I only check literals! What about when earlier arm is more general? For example:\n- Arm 1: `_` (catch-all)\n- Arm 2: `n` (any variable) - should be unreachable\n\nBut the spec says:\n\n> \"**A matched name credits nothing to the certain set either.** Its value is unknown at compile time\"\n\nSo a bare name pattern is not covered in the \"certain set\". This means:\n- Arm 1: `_` covers everything\n- Arm 2: `n` is different from `_` (different patterns)\n- But arm 2 is still unreachable because `_` already matched all values\n\nHmm, so maybe I need to track if there's a catch-all earlier, and if so, mark all later arms as unreachable?\n\nActually, looking at the spec example again:\n\n```csharp\nWhich(a) -> a switch {\n    _  => :any,\n    :x => :ex\n}\n```\n\nThe second arm `:x` is unreachable because the first arm `_` already matched it. So I should check: if there's a catch-all before this arm, it's unreachable!\n\nLet me add this:","time":{"start":1788994831823,"end":1788994837526}}}
